  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 5♣. It seems natural for South to win West's opening spade lead with the ♠A. Dummy's diamonds will eventually provide discards for 2 of South's spade losers. However, the defenders' trumps must first be drawn. South will be unable to avoid losing at least 2 spade tricks and the ♣A.\n\nSuppose South ruffs a spade in dummy, crosses to his hand, and plays clubs. The defense will win the ♣A and cash 2 spade tricks.\n\nSouth does better to duck at trick 1. A spade continuation by West is futile as South can ruff in dummy, preserving the ♠A as a stopper. If West switches to diamonds or hearts, South can win and drive out the ♣A.\n\nEast will return a spade, but South can win the ♠A and draw trump. Dummy's diamonds will provide discards for South's remaining spade losers.",
